// Each element's naturally occurring isotopes, for Mass Spectrum: H to Xe
// (but not Tc, which has none) and Pt, Au, Hg, Pb and U. Each isotope is
// [mass number, relative atomic mass, abundance in %], lightest first.
//
// Abundances are the representative isotopic compositions of the IUPAC
// Commission on Isotopic Abundances and Atomic Weights (CIAAW), "Isotopic
// Compositions of the Elements 2024" (ciaaw.org/isotopic-abundances.htm,
// read 2026-10-09), as given, without their uncertainties. For the elements
// whose 2024 composition is only a range (INTERVAL below), they are the
// single representative values of "Isotopic Compositions of the Elements
// 2009", M. Berglund and M. E. Wieser, Pure Appl. Chem. 83, 397 (2011), as
// NIST lists them; each lies inside its 2024 range, except ³⁶S (0.01%, the
// 2024 range 0.0129–0.0187%).
//
// Relative atomic masses are from J. S. Coursey et al., "Atomic Weights and
// Isotopic Compositions with Relative Atomic Masses", NIST Standard
// Reference Database 144, version 4.1 (2015), physics.nist.gov/Comp, which
// takes them from the 2012 Atomic Mass Evaluation (AME2012).
//
// Every element's abundances add up to exactly 100%, and its weighted
// average mass is within the uncertainty of CIAAW's abridged standard atomic
// weight (2024) (isotopes.test.ts).

/** [mass number, relative atomic mass, abundance in %] */
export type Isotope = readonly [number, number, number]

